From 7b391d35f69d4e5ff5c4cd89b3e70654421c0c08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mminin <mminin2010@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 15:21:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] XFA: Bind fields to data nodes as Acrobat does

Field values stayed in the form and never reached the data DOM, and
field.dataNode was a stand-in that only had saveXML(). Scripts that read
values through data nodes saw nothing: Designer's paper-forms barcodes,
used by four CRA forms, collect their content that way, and failed.

Find a container's data node the way Acrobat binds: by name and
occurrence under its parent's data node, by name anywhere in the record
for global binding, by reference for dataRef (paths such as $.a.b,
$record.a or $data.a.b), not at all for none, with unnamed subforms and
areas transparent. Setting a field's value writes it to its data node,
made if need be, and dataNode gives the real node, brought up to date
with the fields it holds.

Merging data now adds the occurrences of repeatable subforms that the
data has groups for, as the XFA merge does. The consent letter's
language switch saves its data, shows the other language and loads the
data back: conditional sections, such as a child's passport or the trip
contact, have no occurrence until their radio button is answered, so
their data was dropped and half the answers were lost on each switch.
They now survive switching back and forth, and forms such as T1000-3
show the rows their data holds.

Also, for scripts written for Acrobat:
- manifest.evaluate() returns the objects of its references, which may
  end with a property such as dataNode;
- a function a script calls plainly sees the script's object as this,
  since Acrobat runs scripts with their object as the global scope;
- value.text and other content of an empty value read as empty content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
